  "description": "Al-Ghazali's account of how he lost confidence in the certainties he had been taught and how he recovered it: through scholastic theology, which he found insufficient; through philosophy, which he divided into mathematics, logic, natural science, metaphysics and politics; and finally through the Sufi claim to direct experience. It is a first-person map of the intellectual options of the eleventh century.",
  "historicalContext": "Written late in life, after al-Ghazali had left his professorship in Baghdad and travelled to Damascus, Jerusalem and Mecca. The Latin translation circulated in medieval Europe as the Algazel of the philosophers.",
